WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.310
<v Instructor>So now, let's talk about Direct Connect.</v>

2
00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:03.360
So Direct Connect provides you

3
00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.120
a dedicated, private connection from a remote network

4
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.980
into your VPC,

5
00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.320
and the dedicated connection must be set up

6
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:13.980
between data center and the AWS Direct Connect locations.

7
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:15.210
So it's really a private connection,

8
00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:16.440
and therefore, because it's private,

9
00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.030
it's going to be more expensive

10
00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:20.280
than running your own VPN solution.

11
00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.280
And then, when you want to access the services of AWS,

12
00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:27.270
you're going to access them through a private VIF.

13
00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:29.250
Now, it's going to bypass completely

14
00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:30.510
your Internet service provider,

15
00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:33.060
so that means that it goes over private networks.

16
00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.000
So therefore, it's going to reduce your public network cost.

17
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.980
It's going to also to increase the bandwidth

18
00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:39.660
that you have into AWS

19
00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.030
and increase the stability of your connection.

20
00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:44.190
By default, Direct Connect is not redundant,

21
00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:47.070
and so you must set up a failover Direct Connect connection,

22
00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.920
so a second one, or use a VPN as a failover,

23
00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:51.960
which is a very common setup as well.

24
00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.780
So let's talk about the visual interfaces or VIF.

25
00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.460
So you have two kinds of VIF for Direct Connect.

26
00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:57.810
You have the public VIF,

27
00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:01.140
which allows you to connect to public AWS endpoints.

28
00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:03.660
For example, your S3 buckets, your EC2 services,

29
00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:05.250
or anything AWS.

30
00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:06.720
And then, you have a private VIF

31
00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:08.670
to connect to resources within your VPC

32
00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:09.503
that are gonna be private.

33
00:01:09.503 --> 00:01:12.930
So your EC2 instances, your ALB, and so on.

34
00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.630
Then, you have a transit virtual interface,

35
00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:17.940
which allows you to connect to resources in VPC

36
00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:20.070
through a transit gateway.

37
00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:22.770
And if you had a VPC interface endpoint,

38
00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:25.597
they can be accessed through your private VIF.

39
00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.170
So if you look at Direct Connect, how does that work?

40
00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:30.270
Well, we have a region that we want to connect

41
00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:31.260
to a corporate data center

42
00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.060
where we run our customer network,

43
00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:35.460
and then we have a Direct Connect location

44
00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:38.820
that has an AWS cage with a Direct Connect endpoint

45
00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.250
and has also a customer or partner cage

46
00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:43.920
with a customer or a partner router.

47
00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.390
Then, on your own data center,

48
00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:48.240
you're going to run your customer router or firewall.

49
00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.280
And so, in blue is a private VIF,

50
00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:51.930
and in green is a public VIF.

51
00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:54.330
So blue is a private VIF that we connect

52
00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:57.810
from the customer router to the customer or partner router,

53
00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:00.240
to the Direct Connect endpoint

54
00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.490
into a virtual private gateway,

55
00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.170
which allows us, because it's a private VIF,

56
00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:06.540
to connect to the private subnet

57
00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:09.330
and our EC2 instances within it.

58
00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.940
But if you wanted to connect to the public AWS services,

59
00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:15.510
for example, Amazon Glacier or Amazon S3,

60
00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:19.560
then you would require to use a public virtual interface.

61
00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.950
So for this, you would also connect the same way, okay,

62
00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:24.750
all the way to Direct Connect endpoint,

63
00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:26.370
but then the public VIF directly goes

64
00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:29.400
into the AWS services and you're good to go.

65
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:32.070
We have the option of dedicated connections

66
00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:34.260
that range from one Gigabits per second

67
00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:37.620
up to different options, up to 400 Gigabits per second.

68
00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.480
You get a physical Ethernet port

69
00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.400
that's dedicated to a customer,

70
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.740
and the request is made to AWS first,

71
00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:47.130
and then it's completed by an AWS direct partner.

72
00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:49.800
And then, we have a hosted connection

73
00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:52.980
from 50 Megabits per second to 25 Gigabits per second.

74
00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:56.100
Here, we make the connection request directly

75
00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:59.340
via the AWS Direct Connect partners as well,

76
00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:03.720
and the capacity can then be added or removed on demand.

77
00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.210
Oftentimes, to build these connections,

78
00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:07.860
it takes over a month,

79
00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:10.140
so you need to be mindful of this.

80
00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:11.850
So what about encryption on Direct Connect?

81
00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:13.110
Well, by default,

82
00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:16.020
data in transit is not encrypted on Direct Connect,

83
00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:17.310
but it is private to you

84
00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:19.200
because it's using a private connection.

85
00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.570
So if you wanted to also encrypt the data,

86
00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:23.400
then you would need to use Direct Connect

87
00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.710
in combination with a VPN

88
00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:29.310
to get IPsec-encrypted private connection.

89
00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:31.830
Then, if you were to use the VPN of a direct connection,

90
00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:33.990
it will leverage the public VIF,

91
00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:35.640
and you would use the setup

92
00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:37.920
to get an extra level of security,

93
00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.410
but it's slightly more complex to put in place

94
00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:42.030
and may not be as beneficial

95
00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:44.100
as encrypting a public connection.

96
00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:45.090
So let's take an example.

97
00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:47.730
So we have our corporate data center and our VPC,

98
00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:49.320
and then we have the Direct Connect connection

99
00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:50.220
in the middle.

100
00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:51.480
So we establish the connection

101
00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:54.510
and then we would establish a VPN connection over it

102
00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:58.593
to encrypt all the data on your Direct Connect connection.

103
00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:01.950
Now, we also have LAG, or link aggregation groups,

104
00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:03.090
on Direct Connect,

105
00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:04.770
and this is to increase the speed

106
00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:06.150
of your Direct Connect connection

107
00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:07.680
and also allow failover

108
00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:09.900
by summing up existing Direct Connect connections

109
00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:12.090
into one logical bigger one.

110
00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:13.980
So you can aggregate up to four connections

111
00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:15.330
in an active-active mode.

112
00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:18.690
And obviously, you can add connections to the LAG over time.

113
00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:20.160
So if you wanna start by one, then two,

114
00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:21.930
all the way up to four, you can.

115
00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:25.350
So the way it works that we have our Direct Connect location

116
00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:27.120
connected to our corporate data center,

117
00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:28.410
and we'll have one connection setup.

118
00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:30.570
We'll also have a second connection setup.

119
00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:35.040
And then we can create a LAG, so a link aggregation group,

120
00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:38.100
by adding connection one and connection two together.

121
00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:39.840
And similarly, if we have a different

122
00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:42.720
Direct Connect location, location B,

123
00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:45.510
then we can also sum up these connections together

124
00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:48.840
to get a second link aggregation group.

125
00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:50.310
So all the connections in the LAG

126
00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:52.170
must be dedicated connections,

127
00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:54.000
they must have the same bandwidth,

128
00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.797
and they must terminate at the same

129
00:04:55.797 --> 00:04:58.170
AWS Direct Connect connection endpoint.

130
00:04:58.170 --> 00:04:59.730
You can also set, if you wanted to,

131
00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:02.700
a minimum number of connections for the LAG to function.

132
00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:04.920
But, by default, it's going to be one.

133
00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:07.320
So next, we have the Direct Connect gateway.

134
00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:09.330
And the use case is that you wanna set up

135
00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:10.440
a Direct Connect connection

136
00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:13.740
into one or more VPC in many different regions

137
00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:15.660
within the same or across accounts.

138
00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:18.630
In that case, you must set up a Direct Connect gateway

139
00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:20.280
because otherwise it would not be scalable for you

140
00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:21.570
to set up a Direct Connect connection

141
00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:23.760
into each and every region.

142
00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:26.670
So we have two regions here and we have a customer network.

143
00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:28.890
Now, you're going to establish a Direct Connect connection

144
00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:30.420
into one specific region,

145
00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:32.340
but then, you're going to connect the private VIF

146
00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:35.070
into a Direct Connect gateway.

147
00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:37.890
And this gateway is going to talk to the private VIF

148
00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:40.110
of each VPC you're trying to connect to,

149
00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:42.720
and the role of the gateway is going to distribute,

150
00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:44.460
I mean, to allow your Direct Connect connection

151
00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:46.740
to be directly connected to multiple regions,

152
00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:49.950
multiple VPCs in AWS.

153
00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:52.920
Finally, you can also leverage the Direct Connect gateway

154
00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:54.630
with a transit gateway.

155
00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:56.010
So the transit gateway allows you

156
00:05:56.010 --> 00:05:59.880
to link up together many VPCs, VPN connections,

157
00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:01.290
and Direct Connect connections.

158
00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:02.220
And at the very top,

159
00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:05.340
for you to connect your transit gateway to Direct Connect,

160
00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:08.550
you must go through a Direct Connect gateway.

161
00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:09.900
All right, that's it for this lecture.

162
00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:10.980
I hope you liked it.

163
00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:12.930
And I will see you in the next lecture.

